  

<h2 id="while-still-knowing-just-how-much-he-loves-me">While still knowing just how much He loves me</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/3dec4cee-139c-439f-aab6-e2e78f5c53e8/pexels-artempodrez-7048042.jpg" alt="Multimedia depiction of a large whale and a smaller one, on a blue background" width="3000" height="1688"></p>
<p><em>Image by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/whale-cutouts-on-a-blue-surface-7048042/" target="_blank">Artem Podrez,</a> on Pexels</em></p>
<hr>
<p>Someone once said there’s a sure way to know if you are a humble person: If you think you are, you’re not!</p>
<p>I’ve pondered the idea of humility a fair bit, over the years. I do not see it as something to strive for. It’s not a box to tick or an<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>am I there yet?” quality to keep checking in with.</p>
<p>The truth is that we in our earthly state are all painfully humble (closer to the ground — see Charlotte’s Web — than to heaven), but sometimes we forget that. It is far too easy to think we are better than others in one thing or another, or that we deserve better life circumstances than those in which we find ourselves.</p>
<p>Humility is the natural state of things when we are aware of our true standing before God; when we can see ourselves as He does. Our good points, our failures, and the unearned grace He offers to bring us safely back to the Father.</p>
<p>All our false superiority would vanish instantly if we could see just how short we fall from being like God.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One thing the brightest often lack: meekness! Instead of having a willing mind which seeks to emulate the mind of Christ, a mind hardened in pride is impervious to counsel and often seeks ascendancy. Jesus, who was and is<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>more intelligent than they all’ is also more meek than they all.<br>
— Elder Neal A. Maxwell</p>
</blockquote>
<p>May I always be little in my own sight (1 Samuel 15:17), and keep my eyes focused on God through daily, repeated actions that keep Him at the centre of my life.</p>
<p>I strive to spend time — fully present time — in prayer, service, scripture reading, and at the temple. And to actively avoid things that fall outside the bounds the Lord has set, correcting course each time I fall—no matter how many times that might be.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/2c878a7a-823d-495a-9fb1-0a8f2e4bfc01/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260621145503.png" alt="Photo of three pairs of slippers: newborn kitten and bear, bear slippers for a 1-year old" width="1684" height="1100"><br>
<em>Three pairs of slippers for my youngest grandchildren. Kittens for a baby girl we’re expecting next month, brown bears for a darling boy born last month and tan slippers for his brother who earned his angel wings 11 months ago.</em></p>
<hr>
<p>As someone who experiences many of the symptoms reported by people who have been diagnosed with <span class="small-caps">ADHD</span>, I often find it difficult to sit down and focus on the scriptures. What helps is having something to do with my hands while listening to scriptures in audio form. That’s what I did almost exclusively while hand-stitching <a href="https://pkm.social/deck/@ellane/116354764211497267" target="_blank">animal themed slippers</a> for my grandchildren.</p>
<p>The better I get at the things I spend time practicing (whether gospel knowledge, kindness and patience, or making cute slippers), the more the chance I’ll be praised for the outward show of those things.</p>
<p>To avoid straying too far to the left of the pride—humility scale, I can deflect compliments to the kindness of the person offering them, and to God. All good things, abilities, and achievements come because of His grace. And it’s people who take the time to notice the good we do, who make the world a brighter place.</p>
<p>May I always be little in my own sight!</p>
